WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.210
<v Stephane>So now let's talk about Amazon ECS.</v>

2
00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:03.740
And first, what is Docker?

3
00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:05.420
Because Docker is at the center of ECS.

4
00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:07.930
Docker is a software development platform

5
00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:09.070
used to deploy apps.

6
00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:11.400
And apps are going to be packaged in containers.

7
00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.330
And these containers have the specificity

8
00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:15.860
that they can be run on any operating systems.

9
00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:17.270
So apps will run in the same way,

10
00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:19.200
regardless of where they're run.

11
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:20.890
That means that any machine can run Docker container,

12
00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:23.380
there will be no compatibility issues,

13
00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:24.990
and you will have predictable behavior.

14
00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:26.040
That means less work.

15
00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:27.130
They're easier to maintain,

16
00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:28.840
and it works with any language,

17
00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:31.500
any operating system and any technology.

18
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.630
Thanks to this, we can control how much memory

19
00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:36.880
and CPU will be allocated to each container.

20
00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:38.510
And then that allows our container

21
00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:41.570
to use just the right amount of resources.

22
00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:42.760
These containers can be scaled

23
00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:44.440
up and down very quickly within seconds.

24
00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:46.770
Thanks to the Docker management system.

25
00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:49.570
There are therefore for writing applications,

26
00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:52.610
more efficient than virtual machines.

27
00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:53.890
So Docker container management

28
00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:55.800
on AWS can take several forms.

29
00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:57.230
We need a container measurement platform.

30
00:00:57.230 --> 00:00:58.980
So we have either ECS,

31
00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:01.750
which is the Amazon Elastic Container Service,

32
00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:03.630
which is Amazon's own container platform.

33
00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:05.260
Or we can use EKS,

34
00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:07.920
which is Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service.

35
00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.177
And this is Amazon's managed version of Kubernetes.

36
00:01:10.177 --> 00:01:13.020
And Kubernetes is an open source software

37
00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:15.910
that's widely accepted for managing Docker containers.

38
00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:17.060
And then we have Fargate,

39
00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:20.230
which is Amazon's own serverless container platform.

40
00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:24.400
And it works equally well with ECS and EKS.

41
00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:25.860
So let's do a deep dive into ECS.

42
00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:28.800
So ECS, the use cases are to run microservices,

43
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.130
for example, to run multiple Docker container

44
00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:31.430
on the same machine,

45
00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:33.580
or to have service discovery features

46
00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:36.000
to enhance communication between these microservices.

47
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.260
We have a direct integration of ECS containers

48
00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:39.470
with the Application Load Balancer

49
00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:40.990
and the Network Load Balancer.

50
00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:43.140
We have Auto Scaling capabilities.

51
00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:46.040
It's also very helpful if you wanted to run batch processing

52
00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:46.873
or schedule task.

53
00:01:46.873 --> 00:01:49.510
So you can schedule ECS task to run on-demand

54
00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:51.280
on reserves or on spot instances.

55
00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:53.170
And then ECS is also very helpful

56
00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:54.170
when you migrate to the cloud

57
00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:57.170
because you first Dockerize your applications running

58
00:01:57.170 --> 00:01:58.003
on premises,

59
00:01:58.003 --> 00:02:00.350
and then you move these Docker containers to run

60
00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:01.853
on Amazon ECS.

61
00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:04.860
So, concepts you need to know for Amazon ECS,

62
00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:06.190
but hopeful this is not new.

63
00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:07.540
So we have an ECS cluster,

64
00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:10.270
which is a logical grouping of EC2 instances.

65
00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:11.360
We have an ECS service,

66
00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:13.620
which defines how many tasks should run

67
00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:15.080
and how they should be run.

68
00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:16.390
We have the task definition,

69
00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:18.835
which defines what is the JSON form

70
00:02:18.835 --> 00:02:21.270
of the definition of the task to allow

71
00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:23.640
and to tell ECS how to run a Docker container.

72
00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:25.670
So it could be, what is the image name?

73
00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:28.430
How much CPU, how much RAM, et cetera, et cetera.

74
00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:30.560
The ECS task itself is an instance

75
00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:33.010
of a task definition running within a service.

76
00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:35.960
And the ECS task is actually running Docker containers.

77
00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:37.680
And then you have the ECS IAM roles.

78
00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:39.060
So you have two types of roles.

79
00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:40.770
You have the EC2 instance profile

80
00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:42.670
that is used by the EC2 instance

81
00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:45.980
that will be managed by the ECS cluster.

82
00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:48.760
So this EC2 instance is going to make API calls

83
00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:51.850
to the ECS service, send logs, et cetera, et cetera.

84
00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:53.450
But then for each ECS task,

85
00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:55.442
we can attach an ECS task IAM role,

86
00:02:55.442 --> 00:02:58.920
which allows each task to have a specific role.

87
00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:02.080
And these roles are allowing these task to make API calls

88
00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:06.430
to Amazon S3, DynamoDB, and so on.

89
00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:09.900
So if we look at all it in a diagram,

90
00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:12.890
we have a cluster here, we have an EC2 instance,

91
00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:14.920
and we run a service-A.

92
00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:15.910
Now, to run the service-A,

93
00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:18.230
we need to first provide a task definition.

94
00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:21.200
And when this task definition is instantiated,

95
00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:23.340
then we're going to get containers running

96
00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:26.990
on the EC2 instance for that specific service.

97
00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:28.050
So it's possible for you

98
00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:30.160
to scale the number of EC2 instances you have.

99
00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:32.670
So we can add one here and one there.

100
00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:36.900
And then the service may place also ECS tasks

101
00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:38.990
onto these EC2 instances.

102
00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:40.930
Well, because we have multiple EC2 instances,

103
00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:43.470
we can have as well an Auto Scaling group

104
00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:46.850
that will manage these EC2 instances.

105
00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:50.030
And to make sure that these are part of an ECS cluster,

106
00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:52.560
we need to define an EC2 instance profile

107
00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:55.580
that will be allowing our EC2 instances to be registered

108
00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:56.930
to the ECS service,

109
00:03:56.930 --> 00:04:00.050
and also send logs to the CloudWatch logs service.

110
00:04:00.050 --> 00:04:01.890
Now, in case my services

111
00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:05.534
and my task within my services need to access, for example,

112
00:04:05.534 --> 00:04:06.590
on these three buckets,

113
00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:09.350
then we need to make sure to provide the task IAM role

114
00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:11.890
for my task within my service.

115
00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:14.190
And if you wanted to have a second service

116
00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:15.640
within your ECS customer, you could,

117
00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:17.820
and as well as a third and a fifth, et cetera, et cetera.

118
00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:19.840
And so each service will have different tasks

119
00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:21.290
and it will be replaced all

120
00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:24.140
along your available EC2 instances

121
00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:26.140
if there is space for them.

122
00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:28.780
So then Amazon ECS has a great integration

123
00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:30.510
with an Application Load Balancer.

124
00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:33.160
So for this, it's called the dynamic port mapping feature,

125
00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:35.730
which allows us to run multiple instances

126
00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:38.090
of the same application, same Docker container,

127
00:04:38.090 --> 00:04:39.740
on the same EC2 instance.

128
00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:42.500
And the ALB, even though each container

129
00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:44.340
will have a different mapping port,

130
00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:46.570
is able to find the right port on your EC2 instance

131
00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:49.410
and map it to the load balancer.

132
00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:50.700
So let's have an example.

133
00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:52.930
So we have an ECS cluster running one service

134
00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:55.600
and we have four tasks across two EC2 instances.

135
00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:56.900
And so as we can see,

136
00:04:56.900 --> 00:04:59.720
each EC2 instance has two of the same task.

137
00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:01.680
Now, we have users accessing our application

138
00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:03.530
through our load balancer, okay?

139
00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:07.590
And so each ECS task is going to get a different port.

140
00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:09.880
So the first ECS task going to get this port

141
00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:11.680
and the Application Load Balancer is smart enough

142
00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:13.060
to find this port,

143
00:05:13.060 --> 00:05:15.370
thanks to the dynamic port mapping feature.

144
00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:17.848
Then the second ECS task is going to get a different port,

145
00:05:17.848 --> 00:05:20.580
but the ALB is smart enough to find it as well.

146
00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:22.080
And so on.

147
00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:24.340
So even though the port is not stable

148
00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:26.120
across all my ECS tasks,

149
00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:30.050
because the service of ECS is mapped to the ALB,

150
00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:32.320
this dynamic port mapping future comes in

151
00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:36.400
and then the ports are mapped gratefully.

152
00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:38.530
So in terms of use cases,

153
00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:39.780
well, you get increased resiliency

154
00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:41.920
because even if you're running on one EC2 instance,

155
00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:44.350
you can have multiple Docker containers running on it.

156
00:05:44.350 --> 00:05:46.650
You're going to also maximize the utilization

157
00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:49.530
of your CPU and your cores on your EC2 instance.

158
00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:52.250
And if you wanna perform rolling upgrades, you can do it,

159
00:05:52.250 --> 00:05:55.400
because now you have many Docker containers, ECS tasks,

160
00:05:55.400 --> 00:05:56.480
on the one instance,

161
00:05:56.480 --> 00:05:59.560
and therefore, you can take them down one by one

162
00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:03.560
without impacting your overall app uptime.

163
00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:06.610
Okay. So we've seen ECS with EC2 instances.

164
00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:09.060
And so we have to provide EC2 instances ahead of time

165
00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:10.620
to run ECS tasks on them.

166
00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:13.128
But it is possible for you to launch Docker containers

167
00:06:13.128 --> 00:06:14.530
on Fargate.

168
00:06:14.530 --> 00:06:17.620
And with Fargate, we do not provision the infrastructure,

169
00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:19.610
there are no EC2 instances to manage.

170
00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:20.780
Instead it's all serverless.

171
00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:22.410
We don't even know or see

172
00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:24.960
that there are some background hardware.

173
00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:25.793
So how does that work?

174
00:06:25.793 --> 00:06:28.130
Well, the Fargate service runs on ECS

175
00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:31.390
and you can submit Docker containers to a Fargate service.

176
00:06:31.390 --> 00:06:34.040
And the ECS tasks other than Fargate,

177
00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:35.980
are going to be created automatically

178
00:06:35.980 --> 00:06:39.810
without you having you think about EC2 instances at all.

179
00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:41.650
So you just create task definitions

180
00:06:41.650 --> 00:06:45.040
and then you specify how much CPU and RAM you need per task,

181
00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:47.130
and then they will be created for you.

182
00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:48.260
And if you need to scale,

183
00:06:48.260 --> 00:06:49.570
just increase the number of tasks.

184
00:06:49.570 --> 00:06:52.480
Simple, you don't need to manage any EC2 instances

185
00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:53.313
in the backend,

186
00:06:53.313 --> 00:06:55.260
which makes it a very convenient way

187
00:06:55.260 --> 00:06:57.440
of running ECS services.

188
00:06:57.440 --> 00:06:58.860
Now, in terms of security and networking.

189
00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:00.700
So you can inject secrets

190
00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:03.150
and configuration as environment variables

191
00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:04.950
into the running Docker containers.

192
00:07:04.950 --> 00:07:06.580
So there is deep integration

193
00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:08.360
with the SSM Parameter Store

194
00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:10.340
and the Secrets Manager service.

195
00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:12.470
And you also need to know about the different ways

196
00:07:12.470 --> 00:07:14.460
to do networking on ECS tasks.

197
00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:15.440
So the first one is none,

198
00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:18.150
which gives no network connectivity at all to your task.

199
00:07:18.150 --> 00:07:19.580
No port mappings.

200
00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:23.280
Bridge will use Docker's virtual container-based network.

201
00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:25.810
Host will just bypass Docker network,

202
00:07:25.810 --> 00:07:28.590
and will use the underlying host network interface.

203
00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:31.180
And then AWSVPC, which is a special one,

204
00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:34.170
is going to make sure that every ECS task launched

205
00:07:34.170 --> 00:07:37.010
on the instance will get their own elastic network interface

206
00:07:37.010 --> 00:07:38.960
and their own private IP address.

207
00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:41.000
And it's going to give you simplified networking,

208
00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:43.670
enhanced security, security groups,

209
00:07:43.670 --> 00:07:46.030
monitoring VPC Flow Logs,

210
00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:48.450
and then it is going to be the default mode

211
00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:50.610
for Fargate tasks.

212
00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:54.230
Okay. Next, for Amazon ECS, we have Service Auto Scaling.

213
00:07:54.230 --> 00:07:56.870
So this allows you to automatically increase or decrease

214
00:07:56.870 --> 00:07:59.400
the desired number of tasks of your service.

215
00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:00.900
And so for it in the backend,

216
00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:04.350
the ECS service is going to leverage to Amazon,

217
00:08:04.350 --> 00:08:06.870
the AWS Application Auto Scaling Service.

218
00:08:06.870 --> 00:08:08.570
So other general facts,

219
00:08:08.570 --> 00:08:11.570
CPU and RAM are tracked as metrics in CloudWatch

220
00:08:11.570 --> 00:08:13.070
at the ECS service level.

221
00:08:13.070 --> 00:08:15.720
And so therefore we can set up target tracking

222
00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:17.380
to scale based on the target value

223
00:08:17.380 --> 00:08:19.060
of a specific CloudWatch metric,

224
00:08:19.060 --> 00:08:20.650
or we can set step scaling,

225
00:08:20.650 --> 00:08:23.540
or we can set up schedule scaling, and so on.

226
00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:25.640
Just like you would do for an ASG,

227
00:08:25.640 --> 00:08:28.220
but this time for an ECS service.

228
00:08:28.220 --> 00:08:30.690
So even though you can auto scale your ECS service

229
00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:32.660
at the test level and it works really great,

230
00:08:32.660 --> 00:08:35.370
if that ECS service is not running on Fargate

231
00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:38.390
but it is running instead on EC2 instances,

232
00:08:38.390 --> 00:08:39.223
then you should make sure

233
00:08:39.223 --> 00:08:41.350
you also auto scale your EC2 instances

234
00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:44.770
to add more capacity to your ECS cluster.

235
00:08:44.770 --> 00:08:47.440
Therefore, Fargate auto scaling is much easier to set up

236
00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:48.310
because is serverless

237
00:08:48.310 --> 00:08:51.250
and we don't need to think about the underlying resources

238
00:08:51.250 --> 00:08:52.643
to run your auto scaling.

239
00:08:53.900 --> 00:08:56.550
Okay, next we have spot instances.

240
00:08:56.550 --> 00:08:58.430
So spot instances work on ECS.

241
00:08:58.430 --> 00:09:00.890
So for ECS classic, the EC2 launch type,

242
00:09:00.890 --> 00:09:02.650
then we can have underlying EC2 instances

243
00:09:02.650 --> 00:09:05.330
as spot instances managed by an ASG.

244
00:09:05.330 --> 00:09:09.960
And in case the spot instances are going to be removed,

245
00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:11.600
then they may go into draining mode,

246
00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:13.190
which is going to remove the running tasks

247
00:09:13.190 --> 00:09:15.360
and move them over to other instances.

248
00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:16.680
So it's good for cost savings,

249
00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:18.580
but it can impact reliability

250
00:09:18.580 --> 00:09:21.110
because we are using spot instances.

251
00:09:21.110 --> 00:09:22.010
For the Fargate type,

252
00:09:22.010 --> 00:09:26.610
we can also specify a minimum number of on-demand instances

253
00:09:26.610 --> 00:09:28.520
within Fargate, so on-demand tasks.

254
00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:31.630
But we can also add tasks using Fargate Spot

255
00:09:31.630 --> 00:09:32.750
for cost saving,

256
00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:33.880
even though we know that, again,

257
00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:35.680
because there are spot instances,

258
00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:37.133
they can be reclaimed by AWS.

259
00:09:38.060 --> 00:09:40.530
So regardless of on-demand or spot,

260
00:09:40.530 --> 00:09:42.300
Fargate is going to scale really easily

261
00:09:42.300 --> 00:09:44.250
and well based on the load you give it.

262
00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:47.440
Finally, to store these Docker images,

263
00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:50.750
we have Amazon ECR, which is the elastic container registry.

264
00:09:50.750 --> 00:09:53.640
So to store and manage Docker images on AWS,

265
00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:56.130
you have a private registry repository.

266
00:09:56.130 --> 00:09:57.440
So for your own accounts.

267
00:09:57.440 --> 00:09:59.110
But also public repo in case you wanted

268
00:09:59.110 --> 00:10:01.240
to share some images publicly.

269
00:10:01.240 --> 00:10:04.870
And so your repo is going to have multiple Docker images.

270
00:10:04.870 --> 00:10:08.320
Then in case an ECS cluster needs to pull that image

271
00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:09.890
onto EC2 instances,

272
00:10:09.890 --> 00:10:12.500
then thanks to the IAM role of the EC2 instance.

273
00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:15.230
They can pull images from the ECR repository,

274
00:10:15.230 --> 00:10:17.880
and run them as ECS task.

275
00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:20.910
So we have deep integration with ECR and ECS

276
00:10:20.910 --> 00:10:23.470
and there is access control through IAM.

277
00:10:23.470 --> 00:10:25.620
So in case you get permission errors,

278
00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:28.340
then it should be an IAM policy issue.

279
00:10:28.340 --> 00:10:30.780
Now it's possible for you to pull obviously images

280
00:10:30.780 --> 00:10:34.200
from repository A or repository B.

281
00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:35.033
Okay.

282
00:10:35.033 --> 00:10:36.040
And the ECR is really cool

283
00:10:36.040 --> 00:10:38.610
because it supports image vulnerability scanning,

284
00:10:38.610 --> 00:10:42.480
versioning, image tags, and image life cycle.

285
00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:43.920
Okay. So that's a lot on ECS.

286
00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:45.970
I know, but this is necessary for the exam.

287
00:10:45.970 --> 00:10:47.860
